WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:06.020
On February 14th 2019, a suicide bomber attacked
a convoy of Indian security forces.

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:09.240
"We're getting reports of multiple casualties
in a roadside..."

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:12.099
He killed at least 40 Indian soldiers here
in Kashmir.

00:00:12.099 --> 00:00:15.400
"The deadliest attack the region has seen
this century."

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:19.000
The bomber was part of an Islamic militant
group based in Pakistan.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.580
"Jaish-e-Mohammed claimed responsibility for
that attack."

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:26.760
12 days later, India carried out airstrikes
in Northwestern Pakistan.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:31.600
Then Pakistan shot down at least one Indian
aircraft around here.

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:34.760
Tensions rose between the two rival nations.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:36.660
["Escalate" repeated in news broadcasts.]

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:39.100
"The most serious escalation in years."

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.020
Kashmir is one of the most disputed places
on Earth.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:46.340
Over the course of 70 years, it’s been at
the center of three wars

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:48.440
fought between two massive armies.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:53.510
It's heavily occupied by more than half a
million Indian troops and a deadly collection

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:55.340
of militias and terrorist groups.

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:59.640
Kashmir is the stage for the relentless conflict
between India and Pakistan.

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:05.780
But focusing on the two countries can obscure
what's really at stake: The voice of Kashmiris

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:08.300
who are caught in a vicious cycle of violence.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.260
Kashmir is one of the most strategic places in the world,
where 3 powerful countries collide:

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:03.020
India, Pakistan, and China.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:07.580
China invaded and took this slice of Kashmir from India.

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:09.400
And was given this one by Pakistan.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:12.700
India and Pakistan control these parts,

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:14.340
but lay claim to more.

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:20.940
This region is at the center of a brutal conflict over these disputed borders.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:24.600
So it's important to start when they were being drawn.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:29.379
In the mid-1800s, India
was a patchwork of several hundred provinces

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:31.580
and princely states under British rule.

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:37.200
A century later, when British India won independence, the British left and hastily decided

00:02:37.209 --> 00:02:39.020
to split the region into two.

00:02:39.849 --> 00:02:43.269
These areas would be a new Muslim-majority
country, Pakistan.

00:02:43.269 --> 00:02:46.829
And this would be the mostly Hindu, but secular,
India.

00:02:46.829 --> 00:02:48.279
The partition was bloody.

00:02:48.279 --> 00:02:50.989
"Communal hatred flares up in the Punjab."

00:02:50.989 --> 00:02:54.030
"1 million people become refugees overnight."

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:58.720
"They flee from savagery and butchery that has never
been exceeded, even in India's stormy history."

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:03.889
Amid the chaos, some princely states were
given the choice to join either country.

00:03:03.889 --> 00:03:07.480
In most cases, the ruling monarchs followed
the will of their people.

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:10.389
But this state, called "Jammu &amp; Kashmir" was
different.

00:03:10.389 --> 00:03:15.359
It was right along this new border and had
a Muslim-majority population, but was ruled

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:16.880
by a Hindu monarch.

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:21.020
When asked to pick a side, the ruler chose to stay neutral

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:26.960
Fearing that the monarch would join India,
the Kashmiri population rebelled here in 1947

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:30.360
Armed tribesmen from Pakistan soon joined
the fight.

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:35.680
The monarch turned to India for military help
and in exchange agreed to join them, which

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:38.600
sparked the first Indo-Pakistan war in Kashmir.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:47.160
"Continuing thus increased the
threat to world peace and brought the dispute

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.440
to the attention of the United Nations.

00:03:51.209 --> 00:03:56.659
The UN Security Council brokered a ceasefire
in 1949, which established this line with

00:03:56.659 --> 00:04:00.540
Pakistan controlling this side and India this
one.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:05.719
It also asked Pakistani tribesmen to withdraw
and Indian troops to follow, so that Kashmir

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:08.719
could hold a direct vote to decide its own
future.

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:11.620
But neither held up their end of the deal.

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:16.720
Pakistan argued that Kashmir’s Muslim-majority
population rightfully belonged with them.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:20.681
While India insisted that Kashmir was handed over to them by the Hindu monarch.

00:04:20.681 --> 00:04:24.120
So they doubled down and added Kashmir to their constution.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:46.380
Both countries continued to tighten their grip around it for decades.

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:51.460
"Kashmir. Fighting is going on and heavy casualties

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:54.360
in men and equipment have been inflicted on the aggressor."

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:59.400
In 1965, the second India-Pakistan war
broke out in Kashmir.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:03.200
Thousands of people were killed between the
huge armies on both sides.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:06.740
A ceasefire ended the war, but didn’t change
this line.

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:09.820
Kashmir was kept divided and occupied.

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:12.380
And another war broke out in 1971.

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:16.480
This time the focus wasn't in Kashmir — it was in East Pakistan.

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:21.660
Here, India helped rebels fight for independence
and dealt Pakistan a devastating defeat.

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:27.200
This region became a new country, Bangladesh, and Pakistan lost its eastern half.

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:30.130
This made Kashmir more important than ever:

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:34.630
It became one of the most militarized places
on Earth, as India and Pakistan deployed planes,

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:37.560
tanks, artillery, and soldiers along the Line
of Control.

00:05:38.990 --> 00:05:42.820
On the political front, in '87, India reportedly rigged an election,

00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:45.349
declaring a pro-India party as the
winner.

00:05:45.349 --> 00:05:48.200
Now this was a big turning point for many Kashmiris,

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:50.600
who felt they were again denied the chance to vote.

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:53.620
Thousands took to the streets in Indian-controlled Kashmir

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:55.560
to protest the occupation.

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:59.480
But India met the movement for independence with harsh resistance.

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:01.620
Which quickly escalated to more violence.

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:05.460
"In January security forces opened fire on demonstrating separatists,

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:09.700
turning a two-year old struggling movement into a full-blown popular uprising."

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:14.140
"More than 600 people have been killed in clashes between troops and separatists."

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:18.780
Kashmiri militias, like the Jammu and Kashmir
Liberation Front, started recruiting Muslim

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:20.759
youth to fight for independence.

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:23.620
And increasingly attacked the Indian military.

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:27.560
Pakistan saw an opportunity in this insurgency.

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:32.560
They helped introduce a new kind of militant
group: Radical Islamic fighters who fought

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:35.000
for a more pro-Pakistan Kashmir.

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:39.180
By the mid '90s, these groups dominated the
insurgency.

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:45.900
India responded with incredible military
force, deploying 500,000 troops to Kashmir.

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:48.540
And they cracked down on militants and protestors.

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:52.360
Unarmed civilians were killed and many
more were forced to flee the violence.

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:57.080
And in '98 the stakes were raised yet again.

00:06:57.220 --> 00:07:01.360
"Today India conducted three underground nuclear tests."

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:05.220
"Pakistan today successfully conducted five nuclear tests."

00:07:05.420 --> 00:07:09.120
Kashmir became a battleground between two
nuclear-armed nations

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:12.120
and another war broke out in 1999.

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:19.840
"More evidence of the attacks being launched on the Indian-controlled area of Kargil."

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:22.720
"The past two days have seen some of the fiercest
fighting so far."

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:27.900
"Militant Muslim fighters have also crossed over into some parts of Indian-ruled Kashmir."

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:30.880
The 1999 war ended with another ceasefire,

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:33.100
but that didn't stop either country.

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:37.620
Over the years, Pakistan’s militant groups
got bolder and launched terror attacks in

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:39.240
Kashmir and outside of Kashmir.

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:45.060
In 2001, members of Lashkar-e-Taiba bombed
India’s parliament building in New Delhi

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:46.830
killing 14 people.

00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:52.400
And in 2008, 10 militants from  the same group killed 174 people and wounded

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:53.920
300 in Mumbai.

00:07:54.590 --> 00:07:58.320
Meanwhile, Indian military cracked down in Kashmir, firing bullets and

00:07:58.330 --> 00:08:00.610
pellets on unarmed protesters.

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:02.960
Leaving hundreds wounded and blind.

00:08:04.820 --> 00:08:07.419
This is the vicious cycle of violence.

00:08:07.420 --> 00:08:12.100
The Indian Army’s crackdown drives some Kashmiris to join Pakistani-backed militant groups,

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:14.320
who carry out violence against the
Indian forces.

00:08:15.340 --> 00:08:17.940
It’s a cycle that Kashmiri civilians are stuck in the middle of.

00:08:41.060 --> 00:08:42.940
Which brings us back to 2019.

00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:47.770
The suicide bomber was 19-year old Adil Ahmed
Dar from Pulwama, Kashmir.

00:08:47.770 --> 00:08:53.140
According to his parents, in 2016, Indian
police officers stopped him and humiliated

00:08:53.140 --> 00:08:55.660
him by forcing his face into the ground.

00:08:55.660 --> 00:08:58.440
The same year he was shot in the leg at a
protest.

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:04.540
The next year, Dar left home with his brothers,
to join Jaish-e-Mohammed, a Pakistani-supported

00:09:04.540 --> 00:09:08.160
militia that radicalized him and trained him
to be a suicide bomber.

00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:12.620
A year later, he drove explosives into an
Indian military convoy.

00:09:14.260 --> 00:09:19.210
For more than 70 years India and Pakistan
have driven a cycle of violence, retaliation,

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:21.080
and exploitation in Kashmir.

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:24.380
But beneath it all is the Kashmiri's wish to
make a choice.

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:26.640
A wish that continues to

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:29.400
be suppressed, again and again, by violence.

